      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Prediction of Antimatter</strong><br>\nOne of the most significant outcomes of Dirac's equation was the prediction of antimatter. In 1932, Carl Anderson discovered the positron, which is the antimatter counterpart of the electron. This discovery confirmed Dirac's theory and was a major milestone in particle physics. Dirac's prediction showed the power of mathematical beauty in describing the physical world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nobel Prize and Recognition</strong><br>\nFor his groundbreaking work in theoretical physics, Paul Dirac shared the Nobel Prize in Physics with Erwin Schrödinger in 1933. They were honored for their discovery of new productive forms of atomic theory. Dirac was only 31 years old at the time, making him one of the youngest Nobel Laureates in physics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Work on Magnetic Monopoles</strong><br>\nDirac also proposed the existence of magnetic monopoles through his theoretical research. He suggested that the existence of a magnetic monopole could explain why the electric charge is quantized. Although magnetic monopoles have not been discovered, his idea has influenced many areas of research, including string theory and cosmology.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
