          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Later Life and Career</strong><br>\nIn his later career, Dirac held the position of Lucasian Professor of Mathematics at Cambridge University, a post once held by Isaac Newton. He continued to contribute to the field of quantum mechanics and made significant efforts to unify the fundamental forces of nature. Dirac's work continued to be influential until his retirement in 1969.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Personality</strong><br>\nDirac was known for his reserved and solitary personality. He married Margit Wigner in 1937, and they had two children. Colleagues often described him as quiet and with very high standards for precision and logic, both in his personal and professional life. His contributions to physics were deeply rooted in his unique view of the universe's symmetry.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Impact</strong><br>\nPaul Dirac passed away in 1984, but his legacy lives on through his contributions to physics. The Dirac Medal is awarded annually in his honor by the International Centre for Theoretical Physics. He is remembered as one of the pioneers of quantum mechanics and as a physicist who greatly advanced our understanding of the atomic world.</p>\n"
              },
